  "slug": "untitled-topic-c1af",
  "summary": "Population-scale whole-genome, transcriptome, and single-cell cohorts now enable cross-tissue and cell-type-resolved QTL models linked to GWAS.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is that QTL and TWAS inference is conditioned on an explicit structure of biological context, namely tissue, cell-type axis, isoform, or full expression distribution. GBoost-CTL combines single-tissue predictors across tissues, weighting each by out-of-sample accuracy and GWAS signal. BASIC decomposes bulk eQTL effects along cell-type axes to derive axis-QTLs, and distQTL applies Fréchet regression to full single-cell expression distributions in the OneK1K cohort. The whole-blood isoform study uses whole-genome and transcriptome sequencing from FHS and WHI to separate cis and trans irQTLs and resolve isoform-specific regulation. These items share a methodological pattern of embedding context within the association model rather than a common dataset or trait. What is new in each is the mechanism of conditioning: accuracy and GWAS weighting, cell-type axis decomposition, distributional regression, or isoform-ratio mapping.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-11-28"
}
